This is because the radionuclides are frequently obtained by (n, ​

    γ

) neutronic activation of elements (as is the case of 204Tl). The activated targets are solid chemically-pure pieces of the element required. However, this element can contain several stable isotopes also able to be activated. Neutronic activation of each isotope depends on the effective cross-section of the activation reaction, the radioactive half-life of the radionuclide obtained, the neutron flux and the duration of activation.

Secondly, the carrier concentrations in the original solutions provided by different suppliers can be extremely high and very variable from one supply to another, depending on the activity concentration supplied and the mode of production of the radionuclide (for example (n, ​

    γ

) neutronic activation). Preliminary determination of the carrier concentration of the original solution before dilution is therefore recommended.
